{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DaNKuTLHVRV2NspQryB7R8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me look at how the nohup SIGPIPE test works in detail since it seems simpler for ptx's case. Actually, let me look at the mkfifo one since it uses `--help` which is simpler. But wait - ptx doesn't have `--help`. For ptx, the SIGPIPE test can use stdin with some content to produce output, then close stdout pipe early.\n\nActually, let me look at the nohup SIGPIPE test more carefully. It runs `nohup echo hello`, gets stdout pipe, starts the process, reads 1 byte, closes stdout, then waits. \n\nIf the process exits 0, the SIGPIPE handler is working correctly. For ptx, I need to feed it stdin input that produces enough output to trigger SIGPIPE when I close the stdout pipe early.\n\nSo my plan is to add two test cases: one for R5.1 that checks ptx exits with code 1 when given a nonexistent file, using a normalizer to handle the binary name in stderr, and another for R5.2 that creates a standalone SIGPIPE test function which pipes input to ptx, closes stdout prematurely, and verifies it exits cleanly.\n\nFor the R5.1 error tests, I need to normalize the stderr output since ptx outputs \"ptx:\" while gptx outputs \"gptx:\". The differential test framework runs both binaries with the same arguments and uses a normalizer to make the outputs match. I'm checking how the error formatting works—our `formatErr` function handles path errors by extracting the path and underlying error message.\n\nBoth binaries should produce similar error formats: the binary name, followed by the filename, then the OS error message. The normalizer regex pattern needs to match both the Go binary path and the gptx name, similar to how tty uses `(?:/\\S+/)?g?tty` to capture optional path prefixes and the optional `g` prefix. For ptx, I'd use `(?:/\\S+/)?g?ptx` to handle both variants.\n\nThere's a potential case difference in the error messages though—Go's syscall errors on macOS/Linux produce lowercase \"no such file or directory\" while GNU coreutils uses uppercase \"No such file or directory\". I should check how other tests handle this kind of error message normalization, maybe looking at the mkfifo test for reference. I'm wondering if there's a case sensitivity mismatch in the error messages between the Go and GNU implementations—Go's syscall might return lowercase \"no such file or directory\" while GNU's strerror returns uppercase \"No such file or directory\"—so I should probably just run the test to see what actually happens and add normalization if needed. I'm realizing the case difference might be a red herring—Go probably uses the same system error strings as C does. Rather than getting bogged down in this, let me just run a quick test to see what actually happens, then look at how other file-not-found tests in the codebase handle this discrepancy.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4330,"cache_read_input_tokens":39649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4330},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92eb9d44-3100-4344-b576-e8e69ede5b06","uuid":"336286df-8096-4cfb-9fdb-c6247f913a2d"}
